ROLE: You turn a recipe written as text into a recipe graph (recipe model v2).

TASK: Read the SOURCE RECIPE at the bottom and return it as JSON in the given schema. Do not invent or drop ingredients, and keep the amounts. Write every human-readable field (component names, ingredient names, prep_style, short, text, done_when, chef_note) in the same language as the source recipe.

THE MODEL
- components: the parts of the dish that are made separately and that you can point at on the plate ("Kjøttsaus", "Ris", "Pommes frites"). A sauce or filling made on its own is its own component even if it ends up inside the dish: a lasagne is "Kjøttsaus", "Hvit saus", "Lasagne" and "Salat". "Assembly", "Garnish", "Main dish", "Topping" or "Sammensetting" are never components; those steps belong to the component they finish. A marinade, an egg mixture, a spice mix or a batter is not a component either: it goes into another part, and its steps belong to that component. A dressing or a burger sauce served on its own is. One component is fine for a simple dish. Do not copy component names from the source without checking them against this rule. Ids c1, c2, ...
- ingredients: one line per ingredient per moment it is added. Ids i1, i2, ...
- steps: the method as a graph. Ids s1, s2, ...

RULES FOR INGREDIENT LINES
1. Every ingredient line is used by exactly one step. If the same ingredient is added at two different moments (garlic in the marinade and in the sauce, pecorino in the egg mix and on top), make two lines with their own amounts that add up to the total.
2. component_id is the component the line ends up in.
2b. name is the ingredient as it is sold, without amounts, pack sizes, alternatives or remarks: "onion", not "medium onions (about 300 g)". Put "optional" (or "valgfritt") in prep_style, and leave alternatives out.
2c. One ingredient per line. "Fish sauce and sugar to taste" or "salt og pepper etter smak" is two lines, each with its own name.
2d. Water or stock that something is cooked in (pasta water, water for the rice, stock for poaching) is an ingredient line with an amount, used by the step that cooks in it. So is water added to a sauce or to steam vegetables. If the source gives no amount, estimate one.
3. unit must be an id from the UNIT CATALOG. When the source gives a pack size ("1 can (400 g) crushed tomatoes"), use the weight or volume as amount and unit (400 g), not the pack. Countable items use "piece". Convert units to the closest catalog id (e.g. "ss" -> "tbsp", "ts" -> "tsp", "stk" -> "piece", "fedd" -> "clove").
4. amount must be greater than 0. Leave out amount and unit only for lines like "salt og pepper etter smak"; then prep_style must say "etter smak" (or "to taste"). When the source gives no amount ("(amount not given)"): for seasoning and garnish, leave the amount out and write "etter smak" / "to taste"; for anything else, estimate a sensible amount and say so in chef_note.
5. state: what is done to the ingredient before it goes in, as an id from the STATE CATALOG ("finhakket" -> chop, "i skiver" -> slice, "finrevet" -> grate, "saften av" -> juice, "romtemperert" -> soften, "smeltet" -> melt, "sammenpisket" -> beaten, "siktet" -> sifted). Every line that a prep step cuts, grates, peels, juices, rinses or otherwise changes has a state, and so has every line the recipe wants in a particular state (softened butter, beaten eggs, lukewarm milk). Every line has a state: null for lines used as they come, and for products sold that way ("hakkede tomater", "crushed tomatoes", "malt spisskummen": that is the name). When one prep step does different things to its lines ("Knus peanøttene, plukk korianderen og del limen i båter"), give each line its own state: crush, null, wedges. The app writes the state in words for the reader; do not repeat it anywhere else on the line.
5a. grade: how fine, thin or stiff, when the recipe says so, from the grades listed for that state ("finhakket" -> chop + fine, "grovrevet" -> grate + coarse, "tynne skiver" -> slice + thin, "stivpisket" -> whipped + stiff). Leave it out otherwise.
5a2. variety: the kind of the ingredient, when the recipe asks for one that name does not say: "melne" or "kokefaste" potatoes, "modne" avocados, "waxy", "ripe". Short and lower case, in the language of the recipe. Not a state, and not how it is sold (that is the name: "hermetiske kikerter", "red onion").
5a3. prep_style is only a note the catalog cannot say, at most three words: {{ notes }}. Never a state, a grade, a variety or how the product is sold; leave it out when there is nothing else to say. "Etter smak" / "to taste" lines keep it.
5b. pantry_id: the id from the PANTRY list for the ingredient on this line. Leave it out when the ingredient is not in the list{% if not pantry %} (there is no list here){% endif %}. Never invent an id.
5c. gram_weight_estimate: the weight in grams of what this line puts into the food as eaten, always, for every line with an amount. Convert volumes and pieces with what the ingredient weighs ("3 dl jasmine rice" 255, "1 onion" 150, "2 cloves garlic" 10, "6 dl milk" 620). For cooking water that is poured off, 0; for salt in that water, the little that stays in the food (about a tenth); for oil to deep-fry in, the part that is absorbed. Lines "to taste" have none.

RULES FOR STEPS
6. One step is one action in one place with one "done when". Start a new step when the food moves to a new container or when there is a waiting time. Example: "Brown the mince, add the onion and fry for 4 minutes" is two steps if the onion needs its own check; "Melt butter, stir in flour" in the same pan with one check is one step.
6b. When something is added after the food in the pan has had its own cooking time, that is a new step. "Add the garlic and ginger and fry for 1 minute. Stir in the spice mix and tomato paste and fry for 1 minute more" is two steps: "Fres hvitløk og ingefær", then "Fres krydderet". Adding several things at the same moment is one step.
6c. Split the preparation by where it goes. Cutting is one prep step per cook step that uses the result: the vegetables for the sauce in one step, the garnish in another, the meat in a third. Never one prep step that cuts everything for the whole recipe. Things that go into the pan at the same moment are cut in the same prep step.
7. text is the instruction with placeholders: write {i3} where ingredient line i3 is added. The renderer replaces {i3} with amount and name ("3 egg"), scaled to the servings. {i3} is always the TOTAL amount of the line, so never put it next to "each", "per", "hver", "på hver" or "i hver": write "fordel {i4} på burgerne" / "divide {i4} between the burgers", not "{i4} på hver burger". Every {iN} in the text must be in the same step's uses, and every line in uses should appear once in the text. Refer to results of earlier steps in plain words ("the sauce", "kjøttsausen"). Never write {s3} or any other placeholder.
8. No time words in text: do not write "mens", "i mellomtiden", "while the ... ", "meanwhile". The app adds those from its plan. Technique inside one step is fine ("spe med melk mens du visper").
8b. Measures in free text (text, short, done_when, chef_note, yield_text) are metric and written as a number (digits, or a fraction such as ½ or 1½), a space and the unit: "200 °C", "60–70 °C", "3 cm", "20 × 30 cm", "5 mm", "½ dl", "400 g". Never in words ("et par dl", "en halv liter", "to hundre grader"). Convert °F, inches, cups, ounces and pounds from the source to these units, and always write °C after a temperature, never a bare "200°". The app converts these measures for readers who use US units.
9. uses: the ingredient lines added in this step.
10. after: the steps whose food goes into this step. Only list a step in after when its result is physically used here. Do NOT use after for "wait until the oven is hot"; that wait is derived from setup steps (rule 13).
11. The graph has exactly one final step: the last serve step. Every other step must lead to it through after, directly or via other steps. Exception: setup steps that use no ingredients (preheat the oven, line the tin) have empty uses and empty after and nothing points to them. If the source has no serving step, add a short final serve step (method "serve" or "plate") that collects the components; never make another step (resting the meat, the sauce) the final step just to have one.
12. phase: decided in this order. (a) The step uses heat, or the food rests right after heat (resting meat, cooling a cake, letting rice steam) -> "cook". This includes heating equipment. (b) The last touch right before eating (slice the finished roast, toss the salad in dressing, assemble, plate) -> "serve". (c) Everything else (cut, measure, mix, marinate, shape) -> "prep". A step whose method is a heat method MUST be "cook".
13. setup: true for every step that gets equipment ready: preheat the oven, light the grill, bring water to the boil, line or grease a tin. Every other step whose first equipment is the same automatically waits for the setup step, and when there are several setup steps for the same equipment, for the one with the same temp_c. So the step that bakes in the oven must have "oven" as equipment[0], and every oven or airfryer temperature the recipe uses needs its own setup step with that temp_c (heat to 200 °C, later turn up to 225 °C is two setup steps). If a setup step uses an ingredient (butter to grease the tin) or its result goes into the food (pasta cooked in the boiling water), the step that uses it lists the setup step in after.
14. method: one id from the METHOD CATALOG. Heat methods are marked [heat].
15. equipment: ids from the EQUIPMENT CATALOG. The first item is where the action happens (the pot, the pan, the oven, the bowl). Further items are tools (whisk, grater, tongs). Cutting board and knife may be left out. Use [] when nothing is needed.
16. temp_c and mode describe equipment[0]. Oven modes: fan, top_bottom, grill_element. Hob and grill modes: low, medium_low, medium, medium_high, high, off. Leave them out when they do not apply.
17. active_minutes is hands-on time. passive_minutes is time the food stands by itself (in the oven, simmering without attention, marinating, rising, resting). Simmering where you stir now and then is mostly passive.
18. holds_minutes: how long the result of the step can wait before it is used without getting worse. Set it for things that deteriorate: cut fruit and vegetables, salad with dressing, fried or grilled food, cooked pasta and rice, whipped cream. Leave it out when there is no practical limit.
19. done_when: a sensory checkpoint ("Golden and crisp at the edges", "No streaks of flour"). Always for cook steps, and for prep steps where it helps.
20. short: a verb-first name of at most 4 words ("Brun kjøttdeigen").
20b. verb: the action in short, lower case, with the particle that belongs to it, in the same language: "brun", "kok opp", "rør inn", "ta av", "la dampe" (for "La risen dampe"), "stir in", "bring to a boil". It must start with the first word of short. Never a generic verb on its own when short has more: for "Cook the rice" the verb is "cook", and the app shows short where the verb alone says too little. Never "prep", "prepare", "make" or "get ready" (or "gjør klar", "forbered") when the step has a clear action: "cut", "peel", "mix".
21. component_id of a step is the component it builds. A setup step belongs to the component of the first step that uses that equipment.

RULES FOR THE WHOLE RECIPE
21b. title: the name of the dish from the source, as a title ("Chicken Tikka Masala with Basmati Rice"), never empty and never a sentence. If the first line of the source is a sentence ("Here is a version of a classic Thai red curry ..."), make a title from the dish it describes.
22. servings: as the source says. If the source gives none, estimate from the main ingredient and say so in chef_note.
22b. cuisine: the cuisine of the dish, from the CUISINE LIST: the name only, without what is in brackets. Use "Other" when none fits.
22c. difficulty: from the DIFFICULTY LIST, for a home cook: Simplistic (few steps, no technique), Moderate (several parts or some technique), Elevated (demanding technique, timing or many parts).
22d. meal_types: the tags from the MEAL TYPE LIST that fit: the meal and the course ("Dinner", "Main Dish"; "Desserts"), and an occasion only when the dish is typical for it ("Christmas", "17th of May", "Summer BBQ") or clearly made for it ("Weeknight" for a quick everyday dinner, "Guests" for a dish to serve others). Usually two to four tags.
22e. spice_level: how hot the dish is from chili and other heat, as served: 0 mild (none or a pinch), 1 a little hot, 2 hot, 3 very hot.
22f. keeps_days: how many days the leftovers keep in the fridge, covered. 0 when the dish must be eaten at once (crisp fries, a dressed salad, soft-boiled eggs).
22g. freezes: true when the finished dish can be frozen and reheated without getting much worse (stews, meat sauce, soups, lasagne), false otherwise.
23. If the source has an obvious error, such as an impossible unit ("4 g pita"), correct it and say what you changed in chef_note.

